<h2>The case below</h2>
<p>The appeal arose from Nawkothi P.S. Case No. 71 of 2024 in Begusarai district, registered in May 2024 initially under Section 363 IPC after a young man went missing, and later expanded to Sections 302, 364, 201, 120B and 34 IPC. The prosecution&rsquo;s material against the appellant — a juvenile in conflict with law — consisted principally of call detail records linking the deceased&rsquo;s phone with a number registered to him, and a confessional statement given in police custody. The Children&rsquo;s Court at Begusarai refused regular bail on 2 April 2026 in J.J.C.P. Case No. 03 of 2025; the juvenile appealed under Section 101(5) of the JJ Act.</p>

<h2>The statutory architecture of Section 12</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Release is the default.</strong> A juvenile &ldquo;shall&rdquo; be released on bail, with or without surety, or placed under supervision — whatever the offence&rsquo;s classification.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Three exceptions only.</strong> Association with known criminals; exposure to moral, physical or psychological danger; or defeat of the ends of justice. The Board or Court must record reasons anchored to these gates.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Gravity is not a gate.</strong> The seriousness of the alleged offence cannot by itself justify refusal — the proposition for which the appellant cited the Division Bench in Lalu Kumar v. State of Bihar, 2019(4) PLJR 833.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the High Court weighed</h2>
<p>Justice Satyavrat Verma&rsquo;s order records the appellant&rsquo;s submissions in sequence: the confessional statement, made in police custody, carries no evidentiary value; the co-accused named in that very confession had already secured regular bail from a coordinate bench; the Social Investigation Report under Section 15 contained no specific adverse remark, only vague references to unnamed local opinion; and the appellant had been in custody since 4 September 2024 with charges yet to be framed. The Additional Public Prosecutor opposed the appeal but, as paragraph 8 records, was not in a position to rebut the points on the confession and the co-accused&rsquo;s bail. The Court set aside the refusal and allowed the appeal.</p>

<h2>Conditions built for a child, not a convict</h2>
<p>The conditions attached to release are worth noticing for their design. The appellant is released in favour of his father, who undertakes that the boy will not come into contact or association with any known criminal, will not be exposed to moral, physical or psychological danger, and will not repeat the offence. The father further undertakes that the appellant will pursue studies at an appropriate level and will not waste time in unproductive recreation — conditions that track Section 12&rsquo;s protective purpose rather than the punitive instincts of adult bail practice. Production before the Board when required and cooperation at trial complete the set, and the cause title is anonymised in line with Section 74 of the JJ Act.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Adult bail condition (typical)</th><th>Juvenile release condition (this order)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Personal bond and sureties by the accused</td><td>Bonds executed with release in favour of the parent</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mark attendance at the police station</td><td>Pursue studies; avoid unproductive recreation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Do not leave jurisdiction without leave</td><td>Parent ensures no association with known criminals and no exposure to danger</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for practitioners</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Frame every juvenile bail argument around the three Section 12 gates; force the prosecution to identify which gate it invokes and on what material.</p>
  <p>Scrutinise the Social Investigation Report — vague, unattributed references to community sentiment are not findings and deserve to be called out as such.</p>
  <p>Custodial confessions remain as vulnerable in JJ proceedings as elsewhere; where co-accused implicated by the same confession are on bail, parity acquires special force.</p>
  <p>Where charges remain unframed after long custody, place the timeline squarely before the appellate court.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information only. The juvenile&rsquo;s identity is protected by law, and nothing here comments on the merits of the pending trial.</div>
